OAI_PAPER({"slug": "A-Torsion-Free-Group-Algebra-with-Zero-Divisors-September-23-2026", "title": "A Torsion-Free Group Algebra with Zero Divisors", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Group-Algebra-with-Zero-Divisors-September-23-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "196", "family_title": "A counterexample to Kaplansky’s zero-divisor conjecture", "abstract_html": "We disprove Kaplansky's zero-divisor conjecture by constructing a finitely presented torsion-free group <i>G</i> for which \\(\\mathbb F_2[G]\\) has nonzero zero divisors. The group admits a finite two-dimensional classifying space.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem \\(1.1\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There exist a finitely presented torsion-free group \\(G\\) and nonzero elements \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\in\\mathbb F_2[G]\\) such that \\( \\alpha\\beta=0. \\) Moreover, \\(G\\) admits a finite two-dimensional classifying space.", "statement": "<p>Definitions:</p><p class=\"li\">For a field \\(K\\) and a group \\(G\\), the group algebra \\(K[G]\\) consists of finite formal sums of elements of \\(G\\), with multiplication extended bilinearly from the group law.</p><p class=\"li\">A classifying space for \\(G\\) is a connected CW complex with fundamental group \\(G\\) and contractible universal cover.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There exist a finitely presented torsion-free group \\(G\\) and nonzero elements \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\in\\mathbb F_2[G]\\) such that\n\\[\n\\alpha\\beta=0.\n\\]\nMoreover, \\(G\\) admits a finite two-dimensional classifying space.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose the graphs of Proposition \\(4\\), and form the complex \\(X\\) of Proposition \\(5\\). It is finite and two-dimensional. Proposition \\(6\\) makes it a classifying space for the torsion-free group\n\\[\nG=\\pi_1(X),\n\\]\nwhich is finitely presented.</p><p>Let \\(A'\\) be the full vertex set of the component of \\(x_A\\), and \\(B'\\) that of the component of \\(x_B\\). For \\(x\\in A'\\), let \\(g_x\\in G\\) be the label of a path from \\(x_A\\) to \\(x\\). For \\(y\\in B'\\), define \\(h_y\\) using a path from \\(x_B\\) to \\(y\\). These labels are independent of the chosen paths because labels of all closed walks in each component are killed by its cone. Multiply in path order.</p><p>Set\n\\[\n\\alpha=\\sum_{x\\in A'}g_x,\n\\qquad\n\\beta=\\sum_{y\\in B'}h_y^{-1}\n\\quad\\text{in }\\mathbb F_2[G].\n\\]\nBoth sums are finite. The root contributes the identity to each. Proposition \\(5\\) says that no other vertex contributes the identity, so its coefficient is one in each factor. Thus\n\\[\n\\alpha\\ne0,\\qquad\\beta\\ne0.\n\\]\nNo assertion about equality between two non-root labels is needed.</p><p>On \\(A'\\times B'\\), form the graph of simultaneous steps of a common signed label. A step\n\\[\nx\\xrightarrow{t}x',\n\\qquad\ny\\xrightarrow{t}y'\n\\]\nsends \\(g_x\\) to \\(g_xt\\) and \\(h_y\\) to \\(h_yt\\). Therefore\n\\[\ng_{x'}h_{y'}^{-1}\n=\n(g_xt)(h_yt)^{-1}\n=\ng_xh_y^{-1}.\n\\]\nThe reverse step has label \\(\\bar t\\).</p><p>Because full components are used, the degree at \\((x,y)\\) is the entire number \\(|S_x\\cap S_y|\\), which is odd by Proposition \\(1\\). The girth condition excludes loops for the chosen large \\(n\\); parallel edges would not affect the degree-sum argument.</p><p>Every finite connected component of the simultaneous-step graph has an even number of vertices, since its odd degrees sum to twice its number of edges. The value \\(g_xh_y^{-1}\\) is constant on each component. Thus each component contributes zero to the expansion of \\(\\alpha\\beta\\) over \\(\\mathbb F_2\\). Summing over components gives\n\\[\n\\alpha\\beta=0.\n\\]</p><p>This proves the theorem and disproves the universal zero-divisor assertion. In particular, \\(G\\) does not have the unique-product property. The construction is probabilistic: it proves the existence of suitable finite matchings without specifying matchings from which a concrete presentation and zero-divisor factors can be read off.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The construction has the following properties:", "statement": "<p>Prescribed types, random matchings, and turn weights; Sections \\(2.1\\)–\\(2.2\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix\n\\[\nq=128,\\qquad v=q^2+q+1=16513,\\qquad p=\\frac{q+1}{v}.\n\\]\nThe projective plane over \\(\\mathbb F_q\\) has \\(v\\) points and \\(v\\) lines. Each line contains \\(q+1\\) points; each point belongs to \\(q+1\\) lines; two distinct points lie on one common line; and two distinct lines meet at one point.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(T\\) consist of the \\(v\\) projective-plane points, called ordinary letters, and three extra letters. Pair the extras with three distinct ordinary letters, and pair the remaining ordinary letters in twos. Write \\(t\\mapsto\\bar t\\) for the resulting fixed-point-free involution.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(F\\) be the rose with one unoriented edge for each inverse pair in \\(T\\). Thus \\(|T|=16516\\), and \\(F\\) has \\(8258\\) edges.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A,B\\) be disjoint sets of size \\(n\\), where \\(n\\) tends to infinity through multiples of \\(2v^2\\). Prescribe outgoing-label sets \\(S_x\\subset T\\) as follows:</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">1. On each side, the ordinary part of \\(S_x\\) is a line, with every line represented equally often.</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">2. Within each line class on \\(A\\), each of the three two-element extra subsets occurs with proportion \\(p/2\\), and the extra part is otherwise empty.</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">3. Within each line class on \\(B\\), all three extras occur with proportion \\(p\\), and the extra part is otherwise empty.</p><p class=\"li\">Choose roots \\(x_A\\in A\\) and \\(x_B\\in B\\) in advance. For \\(Y=A,B\\), put\n\\[\nY_t=\\{x\\in Y:t\\in S_x\\}.\n\\]\nFor each inverse pair, choose a uniformly random bijection \\(Y_t\\to Y_{\\bar t}\\), using its inverse in the opposite direction. Make these choices independently for all inverse pairs and both sides before conditioning. The resulting graphs are \\(\\Gamma_A,\\Gamma_B\\), and\n\\[\n\\Gamma=\\Gamma_A\\sqcup\\Gamma_B.\n\\]\nA matched pair gives an edge labelled \\(t\\) in one direction and \\(\\bar t\\) in the other.</p><p class=\"li\">A labelled graph immerses into \\(F\\) if each outgoing label occurs at most once at each vertex. An immersed path has no immediate reversal; a closed path is cyclically immersed if this also holds at its last/first junction. Length counts edge traversals, including repetitions. Loops and parallel edges are counted in girth.</p><p class=\"li\">For a non-cancelling turn \\(t,u\\), define \\(w(t,u)\\) to be the frequency of \\(u\\) among vertices of \\(B\\) containing \\(\\bar t\\). Set \\(w(t,\\bar t)=0\\). For \\(W=t_1\\cdots t_h\\), define\n\\[\nP(W)=\\prod_{i=1}^{h-1}w(t_i,t_{i+1}),\n\\qquad\nW^{-1}=\\bar t_h\\cdots\\bar t_1.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The construction has the following properties:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Every letter has frequency \\(p\\) on each side, so \\(|Y_t|=np\\). The graphs immerse into \\(F\\), and their degrees lie between \\(q+1\\) and\n\\[\nd_*=q+4=132.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li num\">2. For every \\(x\\in A\\) and \\(y\\in B\\),\n\\[\n|S_x\\cap S_y|\\text{ is odd}. \\tag{2.1}\n\\]</p><p class=\"li num\">3. For distinct \\(\\bar t,u\\), the turn weights on \\(B\\) are\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|ccc}\n(\\bar t,u)&amp;\\text{ordinary, ordinary}&amp;\\text{ordinary, extra}&amp;\\text{extra, extra}\\\\ \\hline\nw(t,u)&amp;1/(q+1)&amp;p&amp;1\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nwith the mixed entry symmetric. The corresponding conditional frequency on \\(A\\) is at most \\(w(t,u)\\). Every positive weight lies in \\([1/129,1]\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">4. One has\n\\[\nw(t,u)=w(\\bar u,\\bar t),\n\\qquad\nP(W)=P(W^{-1}). \\tag{2.3}\n\\]</p><p class=\"li num\">5. There is \\(\\delta&gt;0\\), and one may take \\(\\delta=1/600\\), such that for every sufficiently large \\(h\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{W\\in T^h}P(W)^2\\le e^{-2\\delta h}. \\tag{2.4}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The projective-plane incidence facts follow by counting one- and two-dimensional subspaces of \\(\\mathbb F_q^3\\).</p><p>The prescribed proportions are integral when \\(n\\) is a multiple of \\(2v^2\\). For example, when \\(n=2v^2\\), each line class on \\(A\\) has \\(129\\) vertices of each double-extra type and \\(32639\\) vertices of the empty type; each line class on \\(B\\) has \\(258\\) vertices of the triple-extra type and \\(32768\\) vertices of the empty type.</p><p>Every ordinary letter belongs to \\(q+1\\) of the \\(v\\) equally represented lines, and hence has frequency \\(p\\). Each extra has frequency \\(p\\) on \\(A\\), since it belongs to two double-extra subsets, each occurring with proportion \\(p/2\\). Each extra has frequency \\(p\\) on \\(B\\) by prescription. Thus \\(|Y_t|=np\\), and the required bijections exist.</p><p>Each outgoing letter occurs exactly once at a vertex carrying it, so the resulting graphs immerse into \\(F\\). Their degrees lie between \\(q+1\\) and \\(q+4\\).</p><p>Two ordinary line parts meet in either \\(1\\) or \\(q+1=129\\) letters. An extra part on \\(A\\) meets an extra part on \\(B\\) in either \\(0\\) or \\(2\\) letters. Therefore every cross-intersection has odd cardinality.</p><p>For distinct ordinary letters \\(\\bar t,u\\), exactly one line contains both, so the conditional frequency is \\(1/(q+1)\\). The mixed conditional frequency is \\(p\\). On \\(B\\), the presence of one extra entails the presence of all three, giving extra-extra conditional frequency \\(1\\). On \\(A\\), the corresponding extra-extra frequency is \\(1/2\\); the other frequencies agree with those on \\(B\\). This proves the asserted domination and the positive-weight range.</p><p>Equal letter marginals and symmetry of joint incidence give\n\\[\nw(t,u)=w(\\bar u,\\bar t).\n\\]\nFor non-cancelling turns, divide the joint frequency of \\(\\bar t,u\\) by \\(p\\); for cancelling turns, both weights are zero. Multiplying along a word gives \\(P(W)=P(W^{-1})\\).</p><p>Finally, Lemma \\(1.1\\) proves the squared-word estimate.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(2.1\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For \\(\\delta=1/600\\) and every integer \\(h\\ge3002\\), \\( \\sum_{W\\in T^h}P(W)^2\\le e^{-2\\delta h}. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the alphabet, involution, turn weights, and word weights of Proposition \\(1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For \\(\\delta=1/600\\) and every integer \\(h\\ge3002\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{W\\in T^h}P(W)^2\\le e^{-2\\delta h}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Let\n\\[\nM_{t,u}=w(t,u)^2,\n\\]\nand set \\(f(t)=5\\) if \\(\\bar t\\) is extra and \\(f(t)=1\\) otherwise. There are precisely three states of weight five.</p><p>If \\(\\bar t\\) is ordinary, the ordinary successors different from \\(\\bar t\\) contribute\n\\[\n\\frac{v-1}{(q+1)^2}=\\frac q{q+1}\n\\]\nbefore the weights \\(f\\) are inserted. At most three such successors have their weight increased by four. The extra successors all have weight one. Hence\n\\[\n(Mf)(t)\\le\n\\frac q{q+1}+3p^2+\\frac{12}{(q+1)^2}\n=\n\\frac{500731261911}{504183783481}\n&lt;\n\\frac{149}{150}.\n\\]</p><p>If \\(\\bar t\\) is extra, the other two extras contribute two; the ordinary successors contribute at most \\(vp^2+12p^2\\). Thus\n\\[\n\\frac{(Mf)(t)}{f(t)}\n\\le\n\\frac{2+vp^2+12p^2}{5}\n=\n\\frac{820350863}{1363395845}\n&lt;\n\\frac{149}{150}.\n\\]</p><p>Consequently\n\\[\nMf\\le\\frac{149}{150}f.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\mathbf1\\le f\\) and\n\\[\n\\sum_t f(t)=16528&lt;e^{10},\n\\]\nwe obtain\n\\[\n\\sum_{W\\in T^h}P(W)^2\n=\n\\mathbf1^{\\mathsf T}M^{h-1}\\mathbf1\n\\le\n16528(149/150)^{h-1}\n\\le e^{-h/300}\n\\qquad(h\\ge3002).\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every sufficiently large admissible \\(n\\), there is a tuple of matchings with \\(\\operatorname{girth}(\\Gamma)\\ge L\\).", "statement": "<p>Girth conditioning, switching, and diameter control; Section \\(2.3\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the prescribed-type matching model of Proposition \\(1\\), with \\(|A|=|B|=n\\), \\(p=129/16513\\), and maximum degree \\(d_*=132\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a positive constant \\(c\\) satisfying\n\\[\nc\\log(2|T|/p)&lt;1,\n\\qquad\n2c\\log d_*&lt;1, \\tag{2.5}\n\\]\nand put\n\\[\nL=\\lfloor c\\log n\\rfloor.\n\\]\nOne may take \\(c=1/100\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A matching prescription specifies one matched pair for one inverse pair on one side. An edge and its reverse describe a single prescription.</p><p class=\"li\">A set of prescriptions is feasible if it is realized by a tuple of matchings satisfying the conditions under consideration.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every sufficiently large admissible \\(n\\), there is a tuple of matchings with \\(\\operatorname{girth}(\\Gamma)\\ge L\\). In the uniform distribution conditioned on this girth event:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. After further conditioning on any feasible set of \\(s\\) prescribed matching pairs, an additional specified pair, not already prescribed in either direction, has probability at most\n\\[\n\\frac1{np-s-r_n},\n\\qquad\nr_n=O(d_*^{2L+2})=o(n), \\tag{2.6}\n\\]\nwhenever the denominator is positive. The error is uniform over all prescriptions.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Every feasible collection of \\(E=O(L)\\) distinct edge prescriptions has probability at most\n\\[\n(np)^{-E}\\exp(o(L)). \\tag{2.7}\n\\]</p><p class=\"li num\">3. There is a fixed constant \\(D_0\\) such that, with conditional probability tending to one, every connected component of \\(\\Gamma\\) has diameter at most \\(D_0L\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma \\(2.1\\) proves nonemptiness and the uniform switching estimate. Applying that estimate successively to \\(E=O(L)\\) distinct prescriptions gives\n\\[\n(np)^{-E}\\exp(o(L)),\n\\]\nbecause the accumulated denominator error is uniform. No independence after girth conditioning is asserted.</p><p>Lemma \\(2.2\\) proves the diameter assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(2.2\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every sufficiently large admissible \\(n\\), the girth-conditioned space is nonempty.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the matching model, \\(c\\), \\(L\\), and matching-prescription conventions of Proposition \\(2\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every sufficiently large admissible \\(n\\), the girth-conditioned space is nonempty. Within its uniform distribution, after any feasible \\(s\\) prescriptions, an additional specified pair not already prescribed in either direction has probability at most\n\\[\n\\frac1{np-s-r_n},\n\\qquad r_n=O(d_*^{2L+2})=o(n),\n\\]\nwhen the denominator is positive, uniformly over the prescriptions.</p>", "proof": "<p>Before girth conditioning, a feasible simple cycle of length \\(k&lt;L\\) has at most\n\\[\n2n^k|T|^k\n\\]\nvertex-and-letter descriptions. Its distinct edges require \\(k\\) distinct matching prescriptions, with probability at most \\((np-k)^{-k}\\). Hence the expected number of edges lying on cycles shorter than \\(L\\) is at most\n\\[\n2\\sum_{k&lt;L}kn^k|T|^k(np-k)^{-k}\n\\le\n2\\sum_{k&lt;L}k(2|T|/p)^k\n=o(n)\n\\]\nfor sufficiently large \\(n\\), by the assumptions on \\(c\\). Choose a tuple with \\(o(n)\\) such bad edges.</p><p>Let \\(xy\\) be a bad edge, oriented with label \\(t\\). Among the \\(np\\) edges for this inverse pair on the same side, choose an edge \\(uz\\), also oriented with label \\(t\\), that is not bad and whose endpoint set is at distance greater than \\(2L\\) from \\(\\{x,y\\}\\). Bad edges exclude \\(o(n)\\) choices, and the maximum-degree ball bound excludes another\n\\[\nO(d_*^{2L+2})=o(n).\n\\]\nThus such an edge exists.</p><p>Transpose the two matches, replacing \\(xy,uz\\) by \\(xz,uy\\). A short cycle using only one new edge would give an old path between the distant endpoint sets. If a short cycle uses both new edges, deleting them leaves old paths pairing the endpoints as either\n\\[\n(x,u),(y,z)\n\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\n(x,y),(u,z).\n\\]\nThe first case again joins the distant sets. In the second case, the \\(u\\)-to-\\(z\\) path together with \\(uz\\) is an old short cycle, contradicting the choice of \\(uz\\). This includes \\(x=y\\), when the \\(x\\)-to-\\(y\\) path may have length zero. No short cycle is created. The chosen bad edge disappears, and the other removed edge was not bad, so the number of bad edges strictly decreases. Repetition produces girth at least \\(L\\).</p><p>Now restrict to a feasible girth-conditioned set with \\(s\\) previous prescriptions, and consider an additional compatible pair \\(xy\\). An incompatible pair has probability zero. From every tuple containing \\(xy\\), perform the same transposition with any sufficiently distant edge \\(uz\\) of the same inverse pair and side that is not previously prescribed. All edges are now nonbad. At least\n\\[\nnp-s-r_n\n\\]\nchoices are available, with a uniform ball-size error \\(r_n\\). Each output preserves girth and the prior prescriptions.</p><p>Given an output and the pair \\(xy\\) to restore, the inverse operation is unique: the current match of \\(x\\) is \\(z\\), and the current preimage of \\(y\\) is \\(u\\). Thus the map from a tuple containing \\(xy\\), together with an admissible transposition, to its output tuple is injective.</p><p>Writing \\(\\Omega_s\\) for the conditioned set and \\(\\Omega_{s,xy}\\) for its subset containing \\(xy\\), we have\n\\[\n|\\Omega_{s,xy}|(np-s-r_n)\\le|\\Omega_s|.\n\\]\nThis proves the probability bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "Lemma \\(2.3\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "With conditional probability tending to one, every connected component of \\(\\Gamma\\) has diameter at most \\(D_0L\\), for a fixed constant \\(D_0\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the uniform matching distribution conditioned on \\(\\operatorname{girth}(\\Gamma)\\ge L\\), as in Proposition \\(2\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>With conditional probability tending to one, every connected component of \\(\\Gamma\\) has diameter at most \\(D_0L\\), for a fixed constant \\(D_0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix a small positive \\(\\rho\\). On either side, require every vertex set of size \\(1\\le k\\le\\rho n\\) to grow to at least \\(2k\\) vertices on adjoining all its neighbors. A failure supplies a set of \\(2k\\) vertices containing at least\n\\[\nr=\\left\\lceil\\frac{(q+1)k}{2}\\right\\rceil\n\\]\ndistinct edges: all edges incident to the failing \\(k\\)-set lie in the larger set and are counted at most twice by its degree sum.</p><p>There are at most \\((C_1n/k)^{2k}\\) choices for the larger set. Its possible typed edge prescriptions number \\(O(k^2)\\); choosing an unordered \\(r\\)-subset costs at most \\((C_1k)^r\\). Decrease \\(\\rho\\) so that \\(r\\le np/3\\) throughout the range. For sufficiently large \\(n\\), Lemma \\(2.1\\) bounds the probability of all \\(r\\) prescriptions by \\((2/(np))^r\\). Consequently the probability of a failure of size \\(k\\), on either side, is at most a fixed multiple of\n\\[\n(C_1n/k)^{2k}(C_1k)^r(2/(np))^r\n\\le\n\\bigl(C_2(k/n)^{62.5}\\bigr)^k,\n\\]\nbecause \\(64.5\\le r/k\\le65\\).</p><p>Choose \\(\\rho\\) still smaller so that \\(C_2\\rho^{62.5}&lt;1/2\\). Summing over \\(k\\le\\sqrt n\\) gives \\(o(1)\\); the sum over larger \\(k\\le\\rho n\\) is at most \\(n2^{-\\sqrt n}\\).</p><p>On the resulting expansion event, every ball reaches more than \\(\\rho n\\) vertices in \\(R=O(\\log n)\\) steps. Along a geodesic, centers separated by \\(2R+1\\) have disjoint radius-\\(R\\) balls. At most \\(1/\\rho\\) such balls fit in one side. This bounds every component diameter by\n\\[\nO(\\log n)=O(L)\n\\]\nwith a fixed constant.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Proposition \\(3.1\\), Bounded-pattern estimate", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a constant \\(\\varepsilon>0\\), depending only on the fixed types and turn weights, with the following property.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the prescribed graphs and fixed roots \\(x_A,x_B\\) of Proposition \\(1\\), with \\(L=\\lfloor c\\log n\\rfloor\\) and the uniform distribution conditioned on \\(\\operatorname{girth}(\\Gamma)\\ge L\\), as in Proposition \\(2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Consider finitely many positive-length paths in \\(\\Gamma\\), all cyclically immersed and closed except possibly one immersed interval path whose initial vertex is \\(x_A\\) or \\(x_B\\).</p><p class=\"li\">An occurrence is a position on one of these paths; different occurrences may traverse the same edge.</p><p class=\"li\">A pairing by interval pairs consists of disjoint occurrence intervals, paired in twos by length-preserving interval isometries. Even when both intervals lie on the same path, they are distinct and disjoint as occurrence intervals.</p><p class=\"li\">An order-preserving comparison requires equal signed letters. An order-reversing comparison requires inverse letters in opposite orders.</p><p class=\"li\">Impose the reduction condition\n\\[\n\\text{paired occurrences traverse distinct underlying undirected edges of }\\Gamma. \\tag{3.1}\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Positions outside the paired intervals are unpaired.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a constant \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), depending only on the fixed types and turn weights, with the following property. For every fixed positive integer \\(K\\), fixed real \\(C\\ge1\\), and fixed nonnegative integer \\(I\\), the conditional probability tends to zero that there exists such a system with at most \\(K\\) paths, total length \\(H\\) satisfying\n\\[\nL\\le H\\le CL,\n\\]\nat most \\(I\\) interval pairs, and \\(b\\le\\varepsilon H\\) unpaired occurrences.</p><p>The constant \\(\\varepsilon\\) is chosen before \\(K,C,I\\), and these three bounds are held fixed as \\(n\\) tends to infinity through its admissible values.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix \\(K,C,I\\). All implicit constants may depend on these bounds, whereas the final choice of \\(\\varepsilon\\) will not. The \\(o(L)\\) and \\(o(s)\\) estimates in the lemmas are uniform over patterns with these fixed bounds. Reversing a chain does not change its word weight, by Proposition \\(1\\).</p><p>For a fixed enlarged pattern, multiply the numerical upper bounds \\(B_j\\) from Lemma \\(3.6\\) over \\(1\\le j\\le m_*\\). Lemma \\(3.2\\) shows that the total exponent of \\(n\\) is nonpositive.</p><p>For a link component \\(\\mathcal U\\) without a self-link, put\n\\[\nd_{\\min}=\\min_{i\\in\\mathcal U}d_i.\n\\]\nIts remaining contribution to the logarithm of the product is\n\\[\nF_{\\mathcal U}\n=\n-\\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal U}m_id_i\n+2z_{\\mathcal U}d_{\\min}\n-\\delta sz_{\\mathcal U}.\n\\]\nWrite \\(S_{\\mathcal U}=\\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal U}m_i\\).</p><p>If \\(2z_{\\mathcal U}\\le S_{\\mathcal U}\\), Lemma \\(3.5\\) gives\n\\[\nF_{\\mathcal U}\n\\le\n-(S_{\\mathcal U}-2z_{\\mathcal U})\\frac{\\delta s}{2}\n-\\delta sz_{\\mathcal U}\n=\n-\\frac{\\delta}{2}sS_{\\mathcal U}.\n\\]\nIf \\(2z_{\\mathcal U}&gt;S_{\\mathcal U}\\), use Lemma \\(3.4\\), the upper bin bound, and \\(z_{\\mathcal U}&gt;S_{\\mathcal U}/2\\) to obtain\n\\[\nF_{\\mathcal U}\n\\le\n(2z_{\\mathcal U}-S_{\\mathcal U})a_0s-\\delta sz_{\\mathcal U}\n\\le\na_0sb_{\\mathcal U}-\\frac{\\delta}{2}sS_{\\mathcal U}.\n\\]\nFor a self-linked component, the negative cost alone satisfies\n\\[\n-\\sum_i m_id_i\\le-\\frac{\\delta}{2}sS_{\\mathcal U}.\n\\]</p><p>Bounded multiplicities and boundedly many chains imply\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\mathcal U}sS_{\\mathcal U}\n=\nH+O(L/s+s)=H+o(L),\n\\]\nand Lemma \\(3.3\\) gives\n\\[\ns\\sum_{\\mathcal U}b_{\\mathcal U}\\le b+o(L).\n\\]\nThere are boundedly many stages by Lemma \\(3.1\\), so their errors remain \\(o(L)\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\log\\prod_{j=1}^{m_*}B_j\n\\le\n-\\frac{\\delta}{2}H+a_0b+o(L). \\tag{3.12}\n\\]</p><p>Choose, once and for all,\n\\[\n\\varepsilon=\n\\min\\left\\{\\frac14,\\frac{\\delta}{16(1+a_0)}\\right\\}&gt;0.\n\\]\nThis depends only on the fixed turn weights. When \\(b\\le\\varepsilon H\\), for all sufficiently large \\(L\\),\n\\[\n\\prod_{j=1}^{m_*}B_j\\le e^{-\\delta H/4}.\n\\]</p><p>Put\n\\[\nM=\\max\\{1,\\lceil2(C+K)\\rceil\\},\n\\]\nso \\(m_*\\le M\\). Some stage satisfies\n\\[\nB_j\\le e^{-\\delta H/(4M)}.\n\\]\nA realization of the whole enlarged pattern requires \\(X_j\\ge1\\) at every stage. Markov's inequality gives\n\\[\n\\Pr(\\text{this enlarged pattern is realized})\n\\le\n\\min_j\\mathbb E X_j\n\\le\n\\min_j B_j\n\\le\ne^{-\\delta H/(4M)}.\n\\]\nNo independence between stages is used: numerical bounds are multiplied solely to select a stage with small expectation.</p><p>There are \\(\\exp(o(L))\\) enlarged patterns by Lemma \\(3.5\\), and \\(H\\ge L\\). The union bound is at most\n\\[\n\\exp(o(L)-\\delta L/(4M)),\n\\]\nwhich tends to zero. This proves the estimate with the asserted order of quantifiers.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The graph \\(\\Delta\\) consists of boundedly many marked chains with disjoint unmarked interiors.", "statement": "<p>The image graph and its bounded chain description.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(K,C,I\\) under Proposition \\(3\\)'s assumptions.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Delta\\) be the actual image subgraph of a candidate path system of total length \\(L\\le H\\le CL\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Mark every path start and end and every vertex whose degree in \\(\\Delta\\) differs from two.</p><p class=\"li\">A marked chain is a path between marks whose interior contains only unmarked degree-two vertices; a chain may return to its initial mark.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(m_e\\) be the total number of traversals of the undirected edge \\(e\\) by the path system.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The graph \\(\\Delta\\) consists of boundedly many marked chains with disjoint unmarked interiors. Multiplicity is constant on each chain, and\n\\[\nm_e\\le2(H/L+K)\\le2(C+K). \\tag{3.2}\n\\]\nThe number of chain traversals is bounded. There are only polynomially many unlabelled descriptions of candidate systems in \\(L\\), where the description includes the marked graph, chain lengths, path traversals, sides, and comparison data, but not vertex names or signed labels. Realizations of such descriptions are required to be injective on vertices and edges.</p>", "proof": "<p>Cutting comparison intervals at the chosen indexing starts of closed paths changes only a fixed bound on the number of interval pairs, so make these cuts at the outset.</p><p>The graph \\(\\Delta\\) has at most \\(CL\\) edges and girth at least \\(L\\). Repeatedly delete leaves and their incident edges, and discard edgeless remnants. This preserves cycle rank. Set aside the components of the resulting core that are circles; there are at most \\(C\\), since each uses at least \\(L\\) edges.</p><p>Suppress all degree-two vertices in the remaining core. The resulting multigraph has minimum degree at least three. Its edges have positive integer lengths whose sum is at most \\(CL\\). Suppose it has \\(r&gt;0\\) edges.</p><p>Start a non-backtracking walk uniformly on its \\(2r\\) directed edges, and at each subsequent step choose uniformly among the allowed continuations. Uniform measure on directed edges is stationary: a directed edge with origin \\(z\\) has \\(\\deg(z)-1\\) allowed predecessors, each contributing\n\\[\n\\frac1{2r(\\deg(z)-1)}.\n\\]\nThis calculation also applies to loops and parallel edges, with degree counted by half-edges.</p><p>The expected expanded length of a walk of \\(k\\) edges is at most \\(kCL/r\\). At least half of the probability lies on walks of expanded length at most \\(2kCL/r\\). Each particular walk has probability at most\n\\[\n(2r)^{-1}2^{-(k-1)},\n\\]\nso there are at least \\(r2^{k-1}\\) such short walks.</p><p>Choose \\(k=O(\\log(r+1))\\) so that this number exceeds \\((2r)^2\\). There are at most \\((2r)^2\\) ordered pairs of endpoint vertices, so two distinct short walks have the same endpoints. Their expansions are distinct reduced paths. Their union contains a cycle, since a tree has only one reduced path between two specified vertices. This cycle has length at most\n\\[\n4kCL/r.\n\\]\nGirth at least \\(L\\) implies \\(r\\le4Ck\\), bounding \\(r\\) in terms of \\(C\\) alone. Thus the cycle rank of \\(\\Delta\\) is bounded.</p><p>The graph \\(\\Delta\\) has no isolated vertices and at most two leaves. A cyclically immersed path cannot visit a leaf, and an immersed interval path can visit one only at an endpoint. If \\(\\beta\\) is the cycle rank and \\(c_\\Delta\\) the number of components, then\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\deg(z)\\ge3}(\\deg(z)-2)\n=\n2\\beta-2c_\\Delta+\\#\\{z:\\deg(z)=1\\}.\n\\]\nHence the number and total degrees of branching vertices are bounded. There are also boundedly many path-start and path-end marks. Every component contains a path start. Suppressing the remaining unmarked degree-two vertices proves the chain bound, including circle components.</p><p>Every path traverses whole marked chains, so \\(m_e\\) is constant on each chain. If an immersed path traverses the same oriented edge twice, the gap between the two starting positions is at least \\(L\\): the intervening segment is a nonempty reduced closed walk and therefore contains a cycle. A path of length \\(h\\) thus traverses a given oriented edge at most \\(h/L+1\\) times. Summing over paths and both orientations gives\n\\[\nm_e\\le2(H/L+K)\\le2(C+K).\n\\]\nThe number of chain traversals is consequently bounded.</p><p>To specify an unlabelled pattern, give:</p><p class=\"li\">the bounded marked multigraph;</p><p class=\"li\">the positive integer length of each chain;</p><p class=\"li\">the side \\(A\\) or \\(B\\) of each component;</p><p class=\"li\">the oriented chain sequence of each path;</p><p class=\"li\">the designation and starting side of the possible interval path;</p><p class=\"li\">the comparison endpoints and their orientation data.</p><p>Each integer endpoint has \\(O(L)\\) choices, and there are boundedly many endpoints. Retain only patterns satisfying the reduction condition. Vertex names and signed labels are not part of the description. The abstract graph is the actual image graph, so subsequent realizations must be injective on vertices and edges. The total number of descriptions is polynomial in \\(L\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "One has \\( \\sum_{j=1}^{m_*}E_j=H, \\qquad \\sum_{j=1}^{m_*}V_j = \\sum_{z\\in\\Delta^{(0)}}\\max_{e\\ni z}m_e \\le H+\\iota. \\) Consequently, \\( \\sum_{j=1}^{m_*}(V_j-E_j-k_j)\\le0. \\)", "statement": "<p>Multiplicity stages and vertex exponents.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a candidate system under Proposition \\(3\\)'s assumptions, with actual image graph \\(\\Delta\\), total length \\(H\\), and edge multiplicities \\(m_e\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n\\[\nm_*=\\max_e m_e.\n\\]\nFor \\(1\\le j\\le m_*\\), let \\(\\Delta_j\\) consist of the edges with \\(m_e\\ge j\\), together with their incident vertices. Write \\(E_j,V_j\\) for its numbers of edges and vertices.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\iota=1\\) if the interval path is present and \\(\\iota=0\\) otherwise. Set \\(k_1=\\iota\\), and \\(k_j=0\\) for \\(j&gt;1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>One has\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=1}^{m_*}E_j=H,\n\\qquad\n\\sum_{j=1}^{m_*}V_j\n=\n\\sum_{z\\in\\Delta^{(0)}}\\max_{e\\ni z}m_e\n\\le H+\\iota. \\tag{3.3}\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=1}^{m_*}(V_j-E_j-k_j)\\le0. \\tag{3.4}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Each edge is counted in exactly \\(m_e\\) stages, and each vertex is counted in exactly \\(\\max_{e\\ni z}m_e\\) stages. This gives the two equalities.</p><p>Let \\(a(z)\\) count interval-path endpoints at \\(z\\), with multiplicity. At a vertex, every traversal incidence except these endpoints is coupled into a passage between distinct half-edges, by immersion and cyclic immersion. The incidences on a half-edge of maximum multiplicity must therefore be coupled to incidences on other half-edges, except for at most \\(a(z)\\) endpoints. Hence\n\\[\n2\\max_{e\\ni z}m_e\n\\le\n\\sum_{\\text{half-edges at }z}m_e+a(z).\n\\]\nSumming over vertices gives the inequality in (3.3), since the half-edge sum is \\(2H\\) and\n\\[\n\\sum_z a(z)=2\\iota.\n\\]</p><p>In stage one, the interval path's initial vertex has its prescribed image, saving one factor of \\(n\\). No such saving is claimed at later stages. Since \\(\\sum_j k_j=\\iota\\), equation (3.4) follows.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a choice \\(1\\le u\\le N^d\\), determined by the unlabelled pattern, such that, with \\( s=\\frac L{uR}, \\) one has …", "statement": "<p>Nearly aligned blocks and a partial matching of block occurrences.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(K,C,I\\) under Proposition \\(3\\)'s assumptions, and an unlabelled pattern with the bounded chain description of Lemma \\(3.1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Orient each marked chain of length \\(\\ell\\), identify it with \\([0,\\ell]\\), and put its edge positions at half-integers.</p><p class=\"li\">Split comparisons at chain-traversal boundaries. The resulting bounded list consists of maps\n\\[\nx\\longmapsto\\sigma x+a,\n\\qquad \\sigma\\in\\{1,-1\\},\\quad a\\in\\mathbb Z.\n\\]\nRelative to chain orientations, these impose equality for \\(\\sigma=1\\) and inverse equality for \\(\\sigma=-1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Pad the offset list by zeros to a fixed length \\(d\\ge1\\). Put\n\\[\nN=\\left\\lfloor L^{1/(2(d+1))}\\right\\rfloor,\n\\qquad\nR=\\lfloor\\sqrt N\\rfloor.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a choice \\(1\\le u\\le N^d\\), determined by the unlabelled pattern, such that, with\n\\[\ns=\\frac L{uR},\n\\]\none has\n\\[\n\\frac L{N^{d+1/2}}\\le s\\le\\frac LR,\n\\qquad\n\\max_a\\operatorname{dist}(a/s,\\mathbb Z)\\le\\frac RN. \\tag{3.5}\n\\]\nThus \\(s\\) tends to infinity at least as a fixed positive power of \\(L\\), while \\(s/L\\to0\\).</p><p>For every chain, use the full intervals \\([ks,(k+1)s)\\subset[0,\\ell]\\). The half-integer positions in each form a full block. Index the underlying full blocks by \\(i\\), and let\n\\[\nh_i=s+O(1),\\qquad m_i=m_e\n\\]\nbe their lengths and chain multiplicities.</p><p>There is a partial matching of full-block occurrences in which every matched pair has a recorded contiguous substring comparison of length at least \\((1-\\kappa)s\\), where \\(\\kappa=o(1)\\) uniformly over the fixed pattern bounds. If \\(b_*\\) is the number of unmatched full-block occurrences and \\(b\\) is the number of unpaired original occurrences, then\n\\[\nsb_*\\le b+O(s+L/s)=b+o(L). \\tag{3.6}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The simultaneous pigeonhole principle gives \\(1\\le u\\le N^d\\) such that\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(ua/L,\\mathbb Z)\\le1/N\n\\]\nfor every offset \\(a\\). Indeed, place the \\(N^d+1\\) vectors of fractional parts of\n\\[\nk(a_1/L,\\ldots,a_d/L),\\qquad 0\\le k\\le N^d,\n\\]\nin the \\(N^d\\) cubes of side \\(1/N\\). Subtract two vectors in the same cube. Choose the least admissible \\(u\\), so all choices are determined by the unlabelled pattern. The displayed estimates for \\(s\\) follow.</p><p>The remaining end fragment of each chain has \\(O(s+1)\\) positions. Every traversal of the same chain uses the same subdivision, including when read backwards.</p><p>Write an offset as\n\\[\na=\\nu s+r,\n\\qquad \\nu\\in\\mathbb Z,\\quad |r|=o(s).\n\\]\nA translation sends the \\(k\\)-th real grid interval to the \\((k+\\nu)\\)-th interval shifted by \\(r\\). A reflection sends it to\n\\[\n((\\nu-k-1)s+r,(\\nu-k)s+r].\n\\]\nBecause \\(a\\) is integral, the isometry preserves the half-integer lattice. In either case, the image overlaps the indicated partner grid block in a contiguous string of at least\n\\[\ns-|r|-2=s-o(s)\n\\]\npositions. The reversed endpoint convention for reflections costs only boundedly many positions.</p><p>Join two full-block occurrences whenever a subcomparison identifies contiguous subwords in them of length at least \\((1-\\kappa)s\\), taking \\(\\kappa=o(1)\\) uniformly large enough to absorb the alignment and rounding errors. Record one such comparison with each join.</p><p>These joins form a partial matching. Each compared substring occupies more than half its block, whereas the original comparison domains are disjoint as occurrence intervals. A block occurrence therefore cannot participate in two joins.</p><p>Only boundedly many blocks lie within \\(3s+O(1)\\) of a subcomparison cut or chain endpoint. Away from these cuts, a full block in a comparison domain has its partner full block and is matched. Every remaining unmatched block away from the cuts consists entirely of unpaired positions. There are \\(O(L/s)\\) block occurrences in total. Rounding their lengths and charging exceptional blocks gives\n\\[\nsb_*\\le b+O(s+L/s)=b+o(L).\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Given a word on any root block in \\(\\mathcal U\\), the number of extensions to all blocks in \\(\\mathcal U\\) satisfying the recorded comparisons is at most \\( \\exp(o(s)|\\mathcal U|). \\) If \\(\\mathcal U\\) has a self-link …", "statement": "<p>Links between underlying blocks.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use a fixed unlabelled pattern and its full blocks of scale \\(s\\) under Proposition \\(3\\)'s assumptions, with the partial matching of block occurrences from Lemma \\(3.3\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Forget occurrence indices in this partial matching. This gives a multigraph whose vertices are the underlying full blocks \\(i\\); its edges are called links. Include isolated blocks.</p><p class=\"li\">Each link has its recorded substring positions and directions fixed by the unlabelled pattern.</p><p class=\"li\">For a connected component \\(\\mathcal U\\) of the link graph, put\n\\[\nS_{\\mathcal U}=\\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal U}m_i,\n\\qquad\nz_{\\mathcal U}=\\max_{i\\in\\mathcal U}m_i,\n\\]\nand let \\(b_{\\mathcal U}\\) be the number of unmatched block occurrences belonging to blocks in \\(\\mathcal U\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. Given a word on any root block in \\(\\mathcal U\\), the number of extensions to all blocks in \\(\\mathcal U\\) satisfying the recorded comparisons is at most\n\\[\n\\exp(o(s)|\\mathcal U|). \\tag{3.7}\n\\]</p><p class=\"li num\">2. If \\(\\mathcal U\\) has a self-link, it has at most \\(\\exp(o(s)|\\mathcal U|)\\) full word assignments altogether, or is infeasible. Reflection self-links are infeasible.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. If \\(\\mathcal U\\) has no self-link, then\n\\[\n2z_{\\mathcal U}\\le S_{\\mathcal U}+b_{\\mathcal U}. \\tag{3.8}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Along a spanning tree of \\(\\mathcal U\\), the recorded comparison specifies \\(s-o(s)\\) letters of each next block word, possibly with reversal and inversion. Since the alphabet is fixed, a root word has at most\n\\[\n\\exp(o(s)|\\mathcal U|)\n\\]\nextensions. Additional links and immersion constraints can only reduce this number.</p><p>If \\(\\mathcal U\\) has a self-link, choose a self-linked block as root. For a translation self-link, the constraints have the form\n\\[\nW(x)=W(x+t)\\qquad(x\\in D),\n\\]\nwhere \\(D,D+t\\) are contiguous subsets of the same block and \\(|D|=s-o(s)\\). The integral shift \\(t\\) is nonzero by the reduction condition: a zero shift would compare the same underlying edge with itself.</p><p>On the \\(h_i\\) positions of this block, the \\(|D|\\) distinct constraint edges form a forest of arithmetic chains of step \\(|t|\\). This forest has\n\\[\nh_i-|D|=o(s)\n\\]\ncomponents, leaving at most\n\\[\n|T|^{h_i-|D|}=\\exp(o(s))\n\\]\nroot words.</p><p>For a reflection self-link, write \\(\\phi(x)=a-x\\). The set\n\\[\nJ=D\\cap\\phi(D)\n\\]\nis contiguous and reflection-invariant, with\n\\[\n|J|\\ge2|D|-h_i&gt;0.\n\\]\nIf \\(a\\) is odd, its center is a half-integer position in \\(J\\), forcing that letter to equal its inverse. If \\(a\\) is even, the two half-integer positions on either side of its center are adjacent positions in \\(J\\), and their letters must be inverse. The first case contradicts the fixed-point-free involution; the second contradicts immersion. Hence reflection self-links are infeasible. Combining the translation bound with the spanning-tree estimate proves the self-linked component bound.</p><p>Finally, suppose \\(\\mathcal U\\) has no self-link. Take a block of multiplicity \\(z_{\\mathcal U}\\). At least \\(z_{\\mathcal U}-b_{\\mathcal U}\\) of its occurrences are matched, and their partners are distinct occurrences of other blocks in the same component. There are only \\(S_{\\mathcal U}-z_{\\mathcal U}\\) such other occurrences. Therefore\n\\[\nz_{\\mathcal U}-b_{\\mathcal U}\n\\le S_{\\mathcal U}-z_{\\mathcal U}.\n\\]\nThis also applies to isolated blocks and to components with multiple links.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.5", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is \\(a_0>0\\), depending only on the turn weights, such that for all sufficiently large \\(L\\), \\( \\frac{\\delta s}{2}\\le d_i\\le a_0s, \\qquad \\#\\{W_i:\\lfloor-\\log P(W_i)\\rfloor=d_i\\} \\le\\exp(2d_i-\\delta s). \\) …", "statement": "<p>Binning the word weights.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(K,C,I\\) under Proposition \\(3\\)'s assumptions and a pattern with full blocks of scale \\(s\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(W_i\\) be the word on full block \\(i\\), read in its chain orientation, and let \\(h_i=s+O(1)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use the word weights \\(P\\) and the constant \\(\\delta\\) from Proposition \\(1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For positive-weight words, define\n\\[\nd_i=\\lfloor-\\log P(W_i)\\rfloor.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is \\(a_0&gt;0\\), depending only on the turn weights, such that for all sufficiently large \\(L\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{\\delta s}{2}\\le d_i\\le a_0s,\n\\qquad\n\\#\\{W_i:\\lfloor-\\log P(W_i)\\rfloor=d_i\\}\n\\le\\exp(2d_i-\\delta s). \\tag{3.9}\n\\]\nEnlarging each unlabelled pattern by its bin integers produces only \\(\\exp(o(L))\\) enlarged patterns, uniformly for fixed \\(K,C,I\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Feasible block words are immersed, so only positive weights need be considered. Proposition \\(1\\)'s squared-word estimate implies\n\\[\nP(W)\\le e^{-\\delta h}\n\\]\nfor every sufficiently long positive-weight word of length \\(h\\). Since the smallest positive turn weight is fixed and positive, the asserted lower and upper bounds on \\(d_i\\) follow.</p><p>Every word in bin \\(d_i\\) contributes more than \\(e^{-2d_i-2}\\) to the sum of squared weights. The same estimate therefore bounds the number of these words by\n\\[\n\\exp(2d_i+2-2\\delta h_i)\n\\le\n\\exp(2d_i-\\delta s),\n\\]\nusing \\(h_i=s+O(1)\\).</p><p>There are \\(O(L/s)\\) blocks and \\(O(s)\\) possible bin values per block. Hence the number of bin choices is at most\n\\[\n\\exp\\!\\bigl(O((L/s)\\log(s+1))\\bigr)=\\exp(o(L)).\n\\]\nTogether with Lemma \\(3.1\\)'s polynomial pattern count, this proves the enlarged-pattern bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.6", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "A realization of the entire path system gives \\(X_j\\ge1\\) at every stage.", "statement": "<p>Expected counts for multiplicity stages.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(K,C,I\\) under Proposition \\(3\\)'s assumptions and an enlarged pattern as in Lemma \\(3.5\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Its actual image graph is \\(\\Delta\\), with edge multiplicities \\(m_e\\). For \\(1\\le j\\le m_*=\\max_e m_e\\), let \\(\\Delta_j\\) have edge and vertex counts \\(E_j,V_j\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\iota\\) indicate the presence of the rooted interval path, and set \\(k_1=\\iota\\), \\(k_j=0\\) for \\(j&gt;1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Each full block \\(i\\) has multiplicity \\(m_i\\), word-weight bin \\(d_i\\), and scale \\(s\\). For a link component \\(\\mathcal U\\), put \\(z_{\\mathcal U}=\\max_{i\\in\\mathcal U}m_i\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A permitted label assignment on \\(\\Delta_j\\) is the restriction of some label assignment on all of \\(\\Delta\\) satisfying the bins, immersion into the rose, and all original comparisons. This is a condition on strings: no embedding of the inactive part of \\(\\Delta\\) is required.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(X_j\\) count injective realizations of \\(\\Delta_j\\) with permitted labels and prescribed sides, and with the prescribed initial vertex in stage one if \\(\\iota=1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>A realization of the entire path system gives \\(X_j\\ge1\\) at every stage. For one fixed permitted stage label assignment, its expected number of vertex realizations is at most\n\\[\nn^{V_j-E_j-k_j}\n\\exp\\left(-\\sum_{i:m_i\\ge j}d_i+o(L)\\right). \\tag{3.10}\n\\]\nThe number of permitted stage label assignments is at most\n\\[\n\\exp\\left(\no(L)+\n\\sum_{\\substack{\\mathcal U\\text{ without a self-link}\\\\z_{\\mathcal U}\\ge j}}\n\\left(2\\min_{i\\in\\mathcal U}d_i-\\delta s\\right)\n\\right). \\tag{3.11}\n\\]\nConsequently, \\(\\mathbb E X_j\\le B_j\\), where \\(B_j\\) is the product of these two upper bounds.</p>", "proof": "<p>A chain is entirely present or absent at stage \\(j\\), because its multiplicity is constant. Let \\(Q_j\\) be the number of active chains. There are \\(E_j-Q_j\\) internal chain vertices.</p><p>If an internal vertex has incoming letter \\(t\\) and outgoing letter \\(u\\), then Proposition \\(1\\)'s turn-frequency bound gives at most\n\\[\nnp\\,w(t,u)\n\\]\npossible images on its prescribed side. Every other unfixed vertex has at most \\(n\\) choices. The fixed initial vertex, when imposed, is marked. Ignoring injectivity bounds the number of vertex assignments by\n\\[\nn^{V_j-k_j}p^{E_j-Q_j}\n\\prod_{\\text{internal chain turns}}w(t,u).\n\\]\nApply probability estimates only to feasible injective choices among these assignments.</p><p>Each such choice requires \\(E_j\\) distinct matching prescriptions. Two distinct edges cannot prescribe the same matching pair in either direction, because injectivity and immersion would identify their outgoing letters at an endpoint. Proposition \\(2\\) bounds the probability of all these prescriptions by\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{1+o(1)}{np}\\right)^{E_j},\n\\]\nuniformly since \\(E_j=O(L)\\).</p><p>Multiplication leaves the bounded factor \\(p^{-Q_j}\\), the power \\(n^{V_j-E_j-k_j}\\), and the product of internal turn weights. Discard turn weights outside full blocks, since they are at most one. The retained product is\n\\[\n\\prod_{i:m_i\\ge j}P(W_i)\n\\le\n\\exp\\left(-\\sum_{i:m_i\\ge j}d_i\\right).\n\\]\nThe bounded factor and prescription errors are absorbed in \\(\\exp(o(L))\\). This proves (3.10).</p><p>For (3.11), consider a link component containing an active block, equivalently \\(z_{\\mathcal U}\\ge j\\). Allowed labels on its active blocks are projections of full feasible word assignments on the component. Their number cannot exceed the number of full assignments.</p><p>If the component has no self-link, choose a root attaining \\(\\min_{i\\in\\mathcal U}d_i\\), count its words by Lemma \\(3.5\\), and extend along a spanning tree by Lemma \\(3.4\\). This is valid even if the chosen root is inactive: it counts full word assignments before projecting and asserts no extension of an embedded graph.</p><p>A self-linked component has only its propagation error, by Lemma \\(3.4\\). Components without active blocks contribute nothing. The sum of propagation errors is \\(o(L)\\), since there are \\(O(L/s)\\) blocks. Arbitrary letters in end fragments contribute at most \\(\\exp(O(s))\\) per marked chain, again \\(\\exp(o(L))\\). Ignoring further constraints between components proves (3.11).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Proposition \\(4.5\\), Absence of reduced spherical arrangements", "depth": 0, "snippet": "With probability \\(1-o(1)\\), \\(\\Gamma\\) admits no reduced spherical arrangement.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the prescribed graphs \\(\\Gamma=\\Gamma_A\\sqcup\\Gamma_B\\), roots \\(x_A,x_B\\), and girth-conditioned distribution of Proposition \\(2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A reduced spherical arrangement, as in the paper's Definition \\(4.1\\), consists of the following data on an oriented sphere:</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">1. Finitely many pairwise disjoint piecewise linear closed disks. Each disk boundary carries a positive-length cyclically immersed closed path in \\(\\Gamma\\), except possibly one boundary. The exceptional boundary carries a positive-length immersed interval path starting at \\(x_A\\) or \\(x_B\\); its endpoints are represented at one marked boundary point, called the break. No immersion condition is imposed across the break. At least one ordinary cyclically immersed boundary is present.</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">2. Paths are read in the boundary orientations induced by the oriented disks. Their edge occurrences are paired in distinct twos by piecewise linear arcs with interiors outside the disks. The arcs are mutually disjoint, and each occurrence contains exactly one arc end in its interior.</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">3. Paired occurrences have inverse signed letters in these boundary orientations and traverse distinct underlying undirected edges of \\(\\Gamma\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Occurrences remain distinct when paths revisit an edge. The arc graph may have loops, parallel edges, or several components.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>With probability \\(1-o(1)\\), \\(\\Gamma\\) admits no reduced spherical arrangement. In particular, for every sufficiently large admissible \\(n\\), there are graphs of the prescribed types with girth at least \\(L\\), the diameter bound of Proposition \\(2\\), and no reduced spherical arrangement.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\) be supplied by Proposition \\(3\\), independently of the fixed pattern bounds. Fix \\(d\\) for the diameter event from Proposition \\(2\\), and then \\(D\\) from Lemma \\(4.1\\).</p><p>Choose constants in the following order:\n\\[\nU\\ge\\max\\{3,\\lceil96D/\\varepsilon\\rceil\\},\n\\qquad U\\in\\mathbb Z,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\eta=\\min\\left\\{\\frac1{32U},\\frac{\\varepsilon}{64U}\\right\\},\n\\qquad\nK=\\max\\{1,\\lceil(C_{\\mathrm{sep}}/\\eta)^2\\rceil\\},\n\\]\n\\[\na=6,\\qquad C_2=32a,\\qquad\nC=K(U+D),\\qquad I=\\lceil C_2C\\rceil. \\tag{4.3}\n\\]\nThese choices imply\n\\[\n\\frac{3D}{U}\\le\\frac{\\varepsilon}{32},\n\\qquad\n2\\eta U\\le\\frac1{16},\n\\qquad\n2\\eta U\\le\\frac{\\varepsilon}{32}. \\tag{4.4}\n\\]\nThe dependency order is\n\\[\n\\text{types},c,\\delta;\\quad\n\\varepsilon,d;\\quad D;\\quad U;\\quad\\eta;\\quad K;\\quad C,I;\\quad n. \\tag{4.5}\n\\]\nAll constants before \\(n\\) are fixed. Proposition \\(3\\) is applied to one fixed triple \\((K,C,I)\\), not bounds growing with \\(n\\).</p><p>Suppose a reduced spherical arrangement exists on the diameter event. Let \\(N\\) be its number of boundaries and \\(H_0\\) their total length. An ordinary cyclically immersed boundary is present, so \\(H_0\\ge L\\).</p><p>Keep every path of length at most \\(UL\\) intact. Split a longer path of length \\(h\\) into\n\\[\nm=\\lceil h/(UL)\\rceil\n\\]\nconsecutive integer-length segments with lengths differing by at most one. For large \\(L\\), their lengths lie between \\(UL/3\\) and \\(UL\\): indeed \\(h/m&gt;UL/2\\), and \\(\\lfloor h/m\\rfloor\\ge UL/3\\) once \\(UL\\ge6\\), while \\(\\lceil h/m\\rceil\\le UL\\). For an exceptional path, use its linear order from the prescribed initial vertex, so no segment spans the break.</p><p>Close each segment by Lemma \\(4.1\\), leaving added occurrences unpaired. Let \\(N'\\) be the number of resulting paths. Each has length at most \\((U+D)L\\) and contains at most \\(UL\\) original occurrences. Since \\(m\\le3h/(UL)\\), the added length satisfies\n\\[\nH_{\\mathrm{add}}\\le\\frac{3D}{U}H_0. \\tag{4.6}\n\\]</p><p>Every resulting path has at least \\(L\\) original occurrences, except possibly the one exceptional path when it is kept intact. Ordinary intact paths satisfy this by girth; split segments satisfy it because \\(UL/3\\ge L\\). Hence\n\\[\nN\\le N',\n\\qquad\nN'L\\le H_0+L\\le2H_0. \\tag{4.7}\n\\]\nIf the exceptional path was long, all its pieces were closed and no interval path remains. Otherwise it remains intact with its prescribed initial vertex.</p><p>Lemma \\(4.2\\) expresses the original pairing by at most \\(aN'\\) interval pairs, each contained in its designated pieces. On a newly closed path, index its original segment first and its closure afterward, keeping these intervals within the linear indexing.</p><p>Make a multigraph whose vertices are the \\(N'\\) pieces and whose edges are the original pairing arcs. It is planar: replace each old disk vertex by separate vertices for consecutive blocks of its incident arc ends, placing these in disjoint sectors of the old disk and joining each to its block of ends within its sector. This introduces no crossings, including for an arc with both ends at one old vertex. Closures have no pairing arcs.</p><p>By Lemma \\(4.3\\), delete at most \\(\\eta N'\\) vertices so that the remaining components, called clusters, each have at most \\(K\\) pieces. The number \\(M\\) of deleted original positions is at most\n\\[\nM\\le\\eta N'UL\\le2\\eta UH_0. \\tag{4.8}\n\\]</p><p>Retain occurrence pairs whose two pieces survive. Each surviving original position has at most one deleted partner, so at most \\(M\\) surviving original positions become unpaired. Each comparison interval is contained in one piece on each side, so an interval pair is retained or discarded in its entirety. No retained pair joins different clusters.</p><p>For a cluster \\(Z\\), let \\(H_Z\\) be its total path length including closures, \\(b_Z\\) its number of unpaired positions, and \\(I_Z\\) its number of retained interval pairs. Then\n\\[\n\\sum_ZH_Z\\ge(1-2\\eta U)H_0,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sum_Zb_Z\\le(2\\eta U+3D/U)H_0,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sum_ZI_Z\\le aN'\\le2aH_0/L. \\tag{4.9}\n\\]\nAlso,\n\\[\nH_Z\\le K(U+D)L=CL.\n\\]</p><p>The retained total length is at least \\(15H_0/16\\). Clusters satisfying \\(b_Z&gt;\\varepsilon H_Z\\) have total length at most\n\\[\n\\frac1\\varepsilon\\sum_Zb_Z\\le H_0/16.\n\\]\nClusters satisfying \\(I_Z&gt;C_2H_Z/L\\) have total length at most\n\\[\n\\frac L{C_2}\\sum_ZI_Z\n\\le\\frac{2a}{C_2}H_0\n=H_0/16.\n\\]\nAfter excluding both classes, at least \\(13H_0/16\\) of cluster length remains.</p><p>A cluster of length less than \\(L\\) contains no positive-length cyclically immersed path. It can therefore consist only of the single intact short exceptional path, and there is at most one such cluster. If its length is \\(h&lt;L\\), the original arrangement also contained an ordinary cyclic path of length at least \\(L\\), so\n\\[\nH_0\\ge h+L&gt;2h.\n\\]\nRemoving this cluster costs less than \\(H_0/2\\). The remaining cluster length is at least\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{15}{16}-\\frac1{16}-\\frac1{16}-\\frac12\\right)H_0\n=\\frac5{16}H_0&gt;0.\n\\]</p><p>Choose one remaining cluster. It has at most \\(K\\) paths and satisfies\n\\[\nL\\le H_Z\\le CL,\\qquad\nb_Z\\le\\varepsilon H_Z,\\qquad\nI_Z\\le C_2H_Z/L\\le C_2C\\le I.\n\\]\nIt inherits the distinct-underlying-edge reduction condition. All paths are cyclically immersed except possibly the intact exceptional interval, still starting at \\(x_A\\) or \\(x_B\\). Added closures may repeat old edges, but all their occurrences are unpaired, as Proposition \\(3\\) permits.</p><p>Thus every reduced spherical arrangement, regardless of size, yields a bounded pattern for the same fixed bounds \\((K,C,I)\\). Proposition \\(3\\) makes the probability of such a pattern \\(o(1)\\), and Proposition \\(2\\) makes failure of the diameter event \\(o(1)\\). This proves the probability estimate and the existence assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(4.2\\), Closing a segment without cancellation", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a fixed integer \\(D\\), independent of \\(n\\) and \\(S\\), such that \\(S\\) extends to a cyclically immersed closed path by appending at most \\(DL\\) edges.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the prescribed graphs with girth at least \\(L\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Add the diameter-event assumption\n\\[\n\\operatorname{diam}(\\Lambda)\\le dL\n\\]\nfor every connected component \\(\\Lambda\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\), where \\(d\\) is a fixed deterministic constant supplied by Proposition \\(2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S\\) be a positive-length immersed segment in \\(\\Gamma\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a fixed integer \\(D\\), independent of \\(n\\) and \\(S\\), such that \\(S\\) extends to a cyclically immersed closed path by appending at most \\(DL\\) edges. The original segment is retained without cancellation. Any integer\n\\[\nD\\ge d+\\frac8{c\\log2}+8\n\\]\nsuffices for all sufficiently large \\(n\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Appending a geodesic return may cancel the ends of the given segment. Prevent this by inserting short based loops with prescribed first and last edge exclusions.</p><p>To construct such a loop at \\(z\\), delete at most two specified incident undirected edges from its side of \\(\\Gamma\\). For sufficiently large \\(n\\), girth at least \\(L\\ge3\\) excludes loops and parallel edges; after deletion, every vertex still has degree at least\n\\[\n(q+1)-2\\ge3.\n\\]\nPut\n\\[\nk=\\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil+1.\n\\]\nFrom \\(z\\), there are at least \\(3\\cdot2^{k-1}&gt;n\\) non-backtracking walks of length \\(k\\) in the remaining graph. Two distinct such walks \\(P,Q\\) end at the same vertex.</p><p>Reduce \\(PQ^{-1}\\) as a linear edge path. This removes just their common terminal segment. Since \\(P,Q\\) have equal length and are distinct, neither is entirely removed. The result is a nonempty reduced based loop of length at most \\(2k\\), whose first and last edges avoid both deleted edges. It need not be cyclically immersed at \\(z\\).</p><p>Let \\(S\\) run from \\(a\\) to \\(b\\), and let \\(R\\) be a geodesic from \\(b\\) to \\(a\\), of length at most \\(dL\\). If \\(R\\) is nonempty, choose a based loop \\(J_b\\) at \\(b\\) excluding the last edge of \\(S\\) and first edge of \\(R\\). Choose \\(J_a\\) at \\(a\\) excluding the last edge of \\(R\\) and first edge of \\(S\\). Then\n\\[\nSJ_bRJ_a\n\\]\nhas no backtracking at any join, including its cyclic last/first join.</p><p>If \\(R\\) is empty, use one based loop \\(J_a\\), excluding the last and first edges of \\(S\\). Then \\(SJ_a\\) is cyclically immersed. The immersion \\(\\Gamma\\to F\\) also makes these joins reduced in signed letters.</p><p>For large \\(n\\),\n\\[\nL\\ge(c/2)\\log n,\\qquad L\\ge1,\n\\]\nand hence\n\\[\nk\\le\\left(\\frac2{c\\log2}+2\\right)L.\n\\]\nThe stated choice of \\(D\\) bounds the added length \\(|R|+4k\\) in units of \\(L\\) and covers the empty-return case.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "Lemma \\(4.3\\), A linear bound on comparison intervals", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The original occurrence pairing can be specified by at most \\(6N'\\) pairs of intervals.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let a reduced spherical arrangement have \\(N\\) disk boundaries.</p><p class=\"li\">Divide its original paths into \\(N'\\ge N\\) pieces by cutting each path into consecutive segments without crossing an exceptional break. An intact path counts as one piece.</p><p class=\"li\">Only original paired occurrences are considered; any edges later added to close segments are left unpaired.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The original occurrence pairing can be specified by at most \\(6N'\\) pairs of intervals. Both intervals in a pair lie wholly in their designated pieces, are distinct as occurrence intervals, and compare inverse strings in reversed boundary order.</p>", "proof": "<p>Thicken the disks and pairing arcs to a regular disk-and-band neighborhood \\(R\\) of their planar multigraph. Let \\(E\\) be the number of arcs. A vertex gap is the boundary arc between consecutive pairing-arc ends around one disk.</p><p>For each connected complementary compact surface \\(Q\\), let \\(\\ell_Q\\) be the number of band sides in its boundary, equivalently the number of intervening vertex gaps with incidences counted separately. Every vertex has positive degree, so every boundary component of \\(R\\) meets a band side, and \\(\\ell_Q\\ge1\\).</p><p>Call a gap good if it belongs to a complementary disk digon whose two sides come from distinct arcs. The two gaps across such a digon are both good. If consecutive occurrences at one corner are \\(e,f\\), with partners \\(e',f'\\), the opposite corner has coherent order \\(f',e'\\). Thus\n\\[\n(e,f)\\longleftrightarrow(f',e')\n\\]\nis an involution on good gaps, continuing an inverse-word comparison.</p><p>Since \\(R\\) retracts to the multigraph,\n\\[\n\\chi(R)=N-E.\n\\]\nGluing complementary surfaces along boundary circles gives\n\\[\n\\sum_Q\\chi(Q)=2-N+E,\n\\qquad\n\\sum_Q\\ell_Q=2E.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\n\\sum_Q(\\ell_Q-2\\chi(Q))=2N-4. \\tag{4.1}\n\\]\nNeither connectedness of the multigraph nor simple connectivity of the complementary regions is needed.</p><p>Bound the non-good gaps as follows.</p><p>A disk monogon has a gap between occurrences paired by its single arc. Those consecutive letters are inverse, so immersion forbids the gap unless it is the exceptional break. There are at most \\(M\\le1\\) monogons, contributing \\(-M\\) to (4.1).</p><p>A disk digon traversing the same arc twice must turn immediately back along that arc at an endpoint. No other arc end intervenes there, so that endpoint has degree one. Different such faces use different degree-one gaps. There are at most \\(N\\) such faces and at most \\(2N\\) gaps of this type.</p><p>Every connected non-disk complementary surface is planar with Euler characteristic at most zero, so its length is at most its contribution \\(\\ell_Q-2\\chi(Q)\\). For a disk region of length \\(\\ell_Q\\ge3\\),\n\\[\n\\ell_Q\\le3(\\ell_Q-2).\n\\]\nAll contributions other than monogons and digons are positive, and their total is \\(2N-4+M\\). Thus the number \\(B_{\\mathrm{gap}}\\) of non-good gaps satisfies\n\\[\nB_{\\mathrm{gap}}\n\\le\nM+2N+3(2N-4+M)\n\\le8N. \\tag{4.2}\n\\]</p><p>Cut at every non-good gap. Also mark one indexing gap per original path, using the exceptional break when present, and all prescribed splitting gaps. There are at most \\(N+N'\\) additional marked gaps: a cyclic boundary split into \\(m\\) pieces needs at most \\(m\\) splitting gaps, and an interval needs no more. Whenever a marked gap is good, cut it and its opposite good gap.</p><p>The resulting cut set is stable under the good-gap involution and has cardinality \\(J\\) satisfying\n\\[\nJ\\le B_{\\mathrm{gap}}+2(N+N')\\le12N'.\n\\]\nEvery original boundary has a cut. Its remaining occurrence-adjacency components are linear runs lying in designated pieces. The pairing reverses every uncut adjacency, and stability of the cut set maps each maximal run onto another maximal run in reverse order.</p><p>A run cannot map to itself. An odd-length run would have a fixed occurrence. An even-length run would pair its two central consecutive occurrences, giving inverse adjacent letters and contradicting internal immersion. The exceptional break was cut and cannot be at that center.</p><p>There are exactly \\(J\\) runs, since each original boundary is a circle with a nonempty cut set. Pairing them gives at most \\(J/2\\le6N'\\) interval pairs with the asserted properties.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.3", "orig": "Lemma \\(4.4\\), Recursive planar separation", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every integer \\(K\\ge1\\), a planar multigraph with \\(m\\) vertices has a set of at most \\( C_{\\mathrm{sep}}m/\\sqrt K \\) vertices whose deletion leaves components of size at most \\(K\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>This lemma concerns an arbitrary finite planar multigraph, without the random-graph or path assumptions of Proposition \\(4\\). Put\n\\[\nC_{\\mathrm{sep}}=\\frac{2\\sqrt2}{1-\\sqrt{2/3}}.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every integer \\(K\\ge1\\), a planar multigraph with \\(m\\) vertices has a set of at most\n\\[\nC_{\\mathrm{sep}}m/\\sqrt K\n\\]\nvertices whose deletion leaves components of size at most \\(K\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Remove loops and merge parallel copies of edges. These operations preserve connected components, including after any vertex deletion.</p><p>The Lipton–Tarjan planar separator theorem supplies, in a simple planar graph with \\(s\\) vertices, at most \\(2\\sqrt{2s}\\) vertices whose deletion leaves components with at most \\(2s/3\\) vertices. Apply this recursively to each component with more than \\(K\\) vertices.</p><p>For a recursive call on \\(s&gt;K\\) vertices, charge \\(2\\sqrt2/\\sqrt s\\) to each vertex. The total charge is \\(2\\sqrt{2s}\\), bounding the number deleted.</p><p>Fix an original vertex and list the successive component sizes containing it before deletion or termination. Consecutive sizes decrease by a factor at most \\(2/3\\). Reading backwards from the last size greater than \\(K\\), the total charge to that vertex is at most\n\\[\n\\frac{2\\sqrt2}{\\sqrt K}\\sum_{j\\ge0}(2/3)^{j/2}\n=\n\\frac{C_{\\mathrm{sep}}}{\\sqrt K}.\n\\]\nThis includes vertices deleted at a separator. Sum over all original vertices. Components already of size at most \\(K\\) incur no charge.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Proposition \\(5.1\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\pi_2(X)=0. \\) Moreover, if a path in \\(\\Gamma\\) starts at \\(x_A\\) or \\(x_B\\) and ends at a different vertex, its label is nontrivial in \\(G\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix graphs supplied by Proposition \\(4\\), with their prescribed roots \\(x_A,x_B\\), girth bound, diameter bound, and absence of reduced spherical arrangements.</p><p class=\"li\">For every connected component \\(\\Lambda\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\), attach its abstract cone \\(K_\\Lambda\\) to the rose \\(F\\) along the labelled map \\(\\Lambda\\to F\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(X\\) be the resulting finite two-dimensional CW complex and\n\\[\nG=\\pi_1(X),\n\\]\nbased at the rose vertex. In particular, labels of all closed walks in \\(\\Gamma\\) are trivial in \\(G\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\pi_2(X)=0.\n\\]\nMoreover, if a path in \\(\\Gamma\\) starts at \\(x_A\\) or \\(x_B\\) and ends at a different vertex, its label is nontrivial in \\(G\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose one conclusion fails. By Lemma \\(5.1\\), the corresponding sphere or disk cone pictures exist. Choose a minimal failure picture as in Lemma \\(5.2\\).</p><p>Lemma \\(5.3\\) proves that paired occurrences traverse different underlying graph edges. The minimized picture is therefore a reduced spherical arrangement: it has at least one ordinary cyclic boundary, at most one rooted exceptional interval with one break, a full planar inverse-letter pairing, and the distinct-underlying-edge reduction condition.</p><p>Proposition \\(4\\) excludes such an arrangement. Hence \\(\\pi_2(X)=0\\), and every path from either prescribed root to a different vertex has nontrivial label in \\(G\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(5.2\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Every essential sphere map to \\(X\\) can be replaced by an essential sphere map admitting a cone picture.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the cone complex \\(X\\) of Proposition \\(5\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A cone picture on a sphere or disk consists of finitely many disjoint tame inner disks in the surface interior. Each inner disk has a specified boundary lift to a component \\(\\Lambda\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\), its filling factors through \\(K_\\Lambda\\), and the complement of the disk interiors maps to \\(F\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Inner boundary lifts are combinatorial closed paths. For a disk domain, the outer boundary additionally reads the label of a specified interval path in \\(\\Gamma\\).</p><p class=\"li\">An essential sphere map represents a nonzero element of \\(\\pi_2(X)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every essential sphere map to \\(X\\) can be replaced by an essential sphere map admitting a cone picture. Every null-homotopy of a graph-path label can be represented by a disk cone picture with that boundary label.</p>", "proof": "<p>For each component \\(\\Lambda\\), choose based edge loops representing a free basis of \\(\\pi_1(\\Lambda)\\). Temporarily replace \\(K_\\Lambda\\) by the space \\(Y_\\Lambda\\) obtained by attaching one disk to \\(\\Lambda\\) along each basis loop.</p><p>The space \\(Y_\\Lambda\\) is simply connected. The attaching-loop classes form a basis of \\(H_1(\\Lambda;\\mathbb Z)\\), so its cellular chains show that its positive-dimensional homology vanishes. A simply connected acyclic CW complex is contractible, by the Hurewicz and Whitehead theorems.</p><p>Both \\(Y_\\Lambda\\) and \\(K_\\Lambda\\) contain \\(\\Lambda\\) as a subcomplex. Their contractibility gives maps between them restricting to the identity on \\(\\Lambda\\), and homotopies of the composites to the identities relative to \\(\\Lambda\\): extend prescribed maps and homotopies over relative cells using the vanishing homotopy groups of the targets. These glue with the identity on \\(F\\), even though \\(\\Lambda\\to F\\) is not injective. We obtain an ordinary rose-with-relator-disks model homotopy equivalent to \\(X\\) relative to \\(F\\).</p><p>That finite model can be triangulated. Take collars inside the relator disks, regard their attachments to \\(F\\) as mapping cylinders, subdivide collar rectangles so their bottom edges map injectively to small rose edges, and triangulate. Approximate a surface map piecewise linearly, preserving its boundary in the graph. If needed, a graph collar homotopy restores the original disk-boundary word.</p><p>Choose a point in the interior of each relator disk outside the images of degenerate or lower-dimensional simplices. It has finitely many preimages, with disjoint disk neighborhoods mapping homeomorphically onto a small target disk around that point. Choose these target disks small enough that there are no other preimages.</p><p>A radial map of each relator disk expands its small target disk to the whole relator disk and sends the remaining annulus to its attaching boundary. This map is homotopic to the identity relative to \\(F\\).</p><p>After composition, the complement of the preimage disks maps to \\(F\\), and each preimage disk reads an attaching loop in one orientation. Return to \\(X\\) using the relative equivalences of the cone substitutes. Each disk then factors through its specified abstract cone. Sphere essentiality and prescribed disk-boundary words are preserved.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a least-total-boundary-length failure picture with:", "statement": "<p>Minimizing a failure picture; Section \\(5.2\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the complex \\(X\\) of Proposition \\(5\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose either:</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">1. \\(\\pi_2(X)\\ne0\\); or</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">2. there are distinct vertices \\(x,y\\) in one component of \\(\\Gamma\\), with \\(x=x_A\\) or \\(x=x_B\\), and a path from \\(x\\) to \\(y\\) whose label is trivial in \\(G\\).</p><p class=\"li\">In the sphere case, allow the essential sphere class to vary. In the disk case, fix the distinct witnessing vertices \\(x,y\\), but allow their connecting interval path to vary.</p><p class=\"li\">Total boundary length counts all inner boundaries and, in the disk case, the outer interval path.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a least-total-boundary-length failure picture with:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Every inner boundary nonempty and cyclically immersed in its specified graph component.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. In the disk case, a nonempty immersed outer interval with the same fixed distinct graph endpoints.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. At least one inner disk.</p><p class=\"li num\">4. After conceptually capping the disk-domain exterior when necessary, an oriented sphere whose boundary occurrences have a complete pairing by mutually disjoint arcs outside the disks, paired letters being inverse in coherent disk-boundary orientations.</p><p>The distinct-underlying-edge reduction condition is not yet imposed.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma \\(5.1\\) makes the appropriate class of pictures nonempty. Choose one of least total boundary length, a nonnegative integer.</p><p>Cyclically tighten every inner loop in its graph component. Perform the tightening on a collar and fill the smaller disk through the same cone. The old filling and replacement are homotopic relative to the old boundary because both factor through that contractible cone. Remove a constant-boundary disk. Minimality therefore gives nonempty cyclically immersed inner boundaries.</p><p>Tighten the outer interval relative to its actual graph endpoints. It remains nonempty because the endpoints are distinct.</p><p>There must be at least one inner disk: a graph has no essential sphere, and a nonempty immersed graph path has a nonempty freely reduced word under the immersion into the rose, so its label cannot be trivial in \\(\\pi_1(F)\\).</p><p>For a disk-domain picture, cap the exterior conceptually by an extra disk. Its boundary carries the interval path, with one marked break from endpoint to initial vertex. No map of that extra disk into \\(X\\) is required. Orient the sphere so that its coherent exterior-disk boundary orientation reads the path from the prescribed root to its endpoint.</p><p>In the region mapping to \\(F\\), take transverse preimages of one interior point in each rose edge. Arrange this by piecewise linear approximation relative to boundary words, using collars to straighten their parametrizations. The preimages are disjoint circles and arcs. Ignore circles. Every letter occurrence has exactly one arc endpoint, giving a complete pairing.</p><p>Transverse orientation over a rose edge gives opposite crossing signs at the two ends of an arc along the oriented boundary of the complement. Passing to coherent disk-boundary orientations reverses both signs. Thus the paired letters are inverse.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is another picture witnessing the same kind of failure with strictly smaller total boundary length.", "statement": "<p>Cancelling a band over the same lifted edge; Section \\(5.3\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use a least-total-boundary-length failure picture from Lemma \\(5.2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose one pairing arc joins occurrences of the same underlying unoriented edge of \\(\\Gamma\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is another picture witnessing the same kind of failure with strictly smaller total boundary length. In the disk case, the retained outer graph path has the same fixed distinct endpoints. In the sphere case, an essential sphere remains.</p><p>Consequently, no such same-underlying-edge pairing occurs in a minimal failure picture.</p>", "proof": "<p>A sufficiently narrow band around the pairing arc maps into the corresponding open rose edge. It lifts to the specified common open graph edge, because that open edge maps homeomorphically to the rose edge. The lift agrees with the specified lifts at both attachment intervals. In particular, any incident inner disks and the band together factor through one common abstract cone.</p><p>Join the incident disks along the band. If the band returns to one disk, the result is an annulus in the sphere. Boundary paths follow the old paths cut at the occurrences and spliced along the band. They lift to the same graph component, except for the retained marked break when the exterior is involved.</p><p>Both spliced joins contract along the common graph edge. Orient it as \\(e:u\\to v\\). The matched occurrences are \\(e\\) and \\(e^{-1}\\), and the local possibilities are\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c}\n\\text{Incident disks}&amp;\\text{Old lifted paths}&amp;\\text{New lifted paths}\\\\ \\hline\n\\text{Two inner disks}&amp;ea,\\ e^{-1}b&amp;ab\\\\\n\\text{Exterior and inner}&amp;aec:x\\to y,\\ e^{-1}b&amp;abc:x\\to y\\\\\n\\text{One inner disk twice}&amp;eae^{-1}b&amp;a,\\ b\\\\\n\\text{Exterior twice}&amp;aebe^{-1}c:x\\to y&amp;ac:x\\to y\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nThe path endpoints are forced by the concatenations. In the last row,\n\\[\na:x\\to u,\\qquad b:v\\to v,\\qquad c:u\\to y,\n\\]\nand the other new boundary is \\(b\\), up to orientation. Thus the break-containing path \\(ac\\) has the original distinct endpoints \\(x,y\\).</p><p>Each band side joins residual edge fragments going from one endpoint of \\(e\\) back to that endpoint through its interior. These fragments contract relative to their endpoints. The two contractions remove the two complete edge occurrences and add no letters. Neither passes through the break. The total boundary length drops by two before further tightening.</p><p>The modifications of the entire picture are as follows.</p><p>If the band joins two distinct inner disks, replace their disk-and-band union by one cone disk. The replacement is homotopic relative to the boundary, since the whole map there factors through the common contractible cone. Tighten its boundary.</p><p>If the band joins an inner disk to the exterior, replace the exterior by their joined disk. Keep the map on the complement and tighten the new outer path relative to the same endpoints.</p><p>If both ends meet one inner disk, remove its annulus with the band and cap the two complementary parts through the same cone. In the disk-domain case, retain the part containing the exterior. In the sphere case, retain an essential capped sphere, whose existence is shown below.</p><p>If both ends meet the exterior disk, the annulus has the break on one boundary component. Retain as the new domain the complementary disk adjacent to that boundary. The last row of the table gives the same outer graph endpoints.</p><p>For essentiality in the inner self-band sphere case, lift the original sphere map to the universal cover \\(\\widetilde X\\). The map \\(K_\\Lambda\\to X\\) has a lift because the cone is simply connected. Choose this lift to agree with the lifted sphere at one point of the annulus. Connectedness and uniqueness of lifts give agreement throughout the annulus. Fill both new caps in \\(K_\\Lambda\\) and use the same lift.</p><p>With compatible orientations, in \\(H_2(\\widetilde X;\\mathbb Z)\\) one has\n\\[\n[S]=[S_1]+[S_2].\n\\]\nThe difference is represented by the annulus together with the reverse caps, all mapping through a contractible cone, and is zero in homology. Since \\(\\widetilde X\\) is simply connected, Hurewicz identifies\n\\[\n\\pi_2(\\widetilde X)\\cong H_2(\\widetilde X;\\mathbb Z).\n\\]\nA nonzero original class therefore leaves a nonzero capped class. No prior vanishing of \\(\\pi_2(X)\\) is used.</p><p>In every case, the retained surface still witnesses the chosen failure and has smaller total boundary length. For the split sphere, the total new length over both parts is already smaller, so retaining one part cannot increase it. Collar homotopies restore combinatorial boundaries; further tightening decreases length. In the exterior cases, the outer path retains the same fixed distinct graph endpoints.</p><p>This contradicts minimality.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Corollary \\(5.3\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The universal cover \\(\\widetilde X\\) is contractible, and \\(G\\) is torsion-free.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(X\\) be the finite two-dimensional cone complex of Proposition \\(5\\), and put \\(G=\\pi_1(X)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The universal cover \\(\\widetilde X\\) is contractible, and \\(G\\) is torsion-free.</p>", "proof": "<p>The simply connected two-dimensional cover \\(\\widetilde X\\) has \\(H_2=0\\) by Proposition \\(5\\) and Hurewicz, has \\(H_1=0\\), and has no cellular homology above dimension two. The homological form of Whitehead's theorem therefore makes it contractible.</p><p>For the torsion obstruction, the augmented cellular chains of \\(\\widetilde X\\) give an exact free \\(\\mathbb ZG\\)-resolution of \\(\\mathbb Z\\) of length two.</p><p>If \\(G\\) had a nontrivial finite-order element, a power would generate a cyclic subgroup\n\\[\nH=\\langle g\\rangle\n\\]\nof prime order \\(\\ell\\). Restricting the resolution to \\(\\mathbb ZH\\) preserves freeness, since \\(\\mathbb ZG\\) is free as a left \\(\\mathbb ZH\\)-module on coset representatives.</p><p>The cyclic group has the exact periodic resolution\n\\[\n\\cdots\n\\xrightarrow{\\,N\\,}\\mathbb ZH\n\\xrightarrow{\\,g-1\\,}\\mathbb ZH\n\\xrightarrow{\\,N\\,}\\mathbb ZH\n\\xrightarrow{\\,g-1\\,}\\mathbb ZH\n\\longrightarrow\\mathbb Z\\longrightarrow0,\n\\qquad\nN=1+g+\\cdots+g^{\\ell-1}.\n\\]\nExactness follows by comparing coefficients of \\(1,g,\\ldots,g^{\\ell-1}\\): the kernel of \\(g-1\\) consists of multiples of \\(N\\), and the kernel of \\(N\\) is the augmentation ideal generated by \\(g-1\\).</p><p>With trivial coefficients \\(\\mathbb F_\\ell\\), all differentials in the corresponding cochain complex vanish. Hence\n\\[\nH^k(H;\\mathbb F_\\ell)\\cong\\mathbb F_\\ell\n\\]\nin every degree. The comparison theorem for projective resolutions makes this cohomology independent of the chosen resolution. This contradicts the vanishing above degree two forced by the restricted finite-length free resolution. Therefore \\(G\\) is torsion-free.</p>"}], "edges": [[2, 1, "part"], [4, 3, "part"], [5, 3, "part"], [7, 6, "part"], [8, 6, "part"], [9, 6, "part"], [10, 6, "part"], [11, 6, "part"], [12, 6, "part"], [14, 13, "part"], [15, 13, "part"], [16, 13, "part"], [18, 17, "part"], [19, 17, "part"], [20, 17, "part"]]});
